title: Story_01_Intro
---
Terminal: NEW ASSIGNMENT RECEIVED. COORDINATES ATTACHED.
Terminal: SALVAGE PRIORITY: LOW. QUOTA DEADLINE: 40 CYCLES.
Terminal: SENTENCE STATUS: SUSPENDED. REVIEW PENDING ON QUOTA.
Jim: "Pending." They love that word.
Jim: Alright. Time to move.
===

title: Story_02_Departure
---
Jim: Course locked. Nothing out here but me and the dark.
Jim: Let's see what the void left me this time.
===

title: Story_03_Arrival
---
Jim: These are the coordinates. Whatever's left of it, it's right here.
Jim: Hooks out. Let's get this over with.
===

title: Story_04_Haul
---
Jim: ...Scrap. Half a ton of scrap and a cracked coolant cell.
Jim: Months. Months since I pulled anything worth the fuel.
Jim: The Corporation doesn't fire people. It just stops suspending the sentence.
Jim: Keep this up and I don't lose the job. I lose my head.
Jim: How did I even end up out here.
===

title: Flashback_01_Cells
---
Guard: Block four. Every one of them scheduled.
Suit: Scheduled is fine. Scheduled is what makes them affordable.
Suit: These will do. Walk them to the assembly hall.
Guard: All of them?
Suit: All of them. The ones who decline can be scheduled again.
===

title: Flashback_02_Assembly
---
Suit: Look around this room. Every man here has a date and a time.
Suit: The Corporation is offering you something the court would not. A delay.
Suit: You will each be given a ship and a sector. The deep field — the part of space we do not send employees into.
Suit: You bring back salvage. You meet quota. And for as long as you meet it, your date stays where it is. Ahead of you.
Suit: Haul enough, long enough, and you may even buy the rest of your life back.
===

title: Flashback_03_Outburst
---
Prisoner: Buy it back. From the people who bought it.
Prisoner: Keep your delay. Do it here and save yourself the fuel.
Suit: Stay in line.
Prisoner: I'm finished listening.
===

title: Flashback_04_Verdict
---
Suit: As you can see, there is exactly one way out of this hall.
Suit: Your lives were sold. That transaction is closed; it is not what we are discussing today.
Suit: The only question still open is whether we consider them worth keeping.
Suit: Congratulations. You are assets now.
===

title: Story_05_Contact
---
Terminal: PROXIMITY ALERT. VESSEL ON DRIFT COURSE.
Terminal: TRANSPONDER: INDEPENDENT SALVAGE. HAILING CHANNEL OPEN.
Jim: Someone out here actually wants to talk. That's new.
===

title: Scavenger_Encounter
---
<<if attitude("Vee") >= 25>>
    <<jump Scavenger_Encounter_Friendly>>
<<else>>
    <<jump Scavenger_Encounter_Hostile>>
<<endif>>
===

title: Scavenger_Encounter_Friendly
---
Vee: Hey! How've you been? Pulled anything worth hauling lately?
Vee: Ever since they raised the quotas, this job has been a grind.
Vee: I upgraded my radar. Now I get more coordinates than I can fly to — you should stop putting that off.
Vee: Here, take one. Two pings came in at once, opposite ends of the sector. Someone's going to beat me to the other one anyway. Better it's you.
-> Thanks. I owe you one.
    <<apply_effect Attitude_Vee_Plus_5>>
    Vee: Just don't get yourself blown up out there.
-> Why hand it over?
    Vee: Because I can't be in two places. And I'd rather it went to you than to some Corporation lapdog.
===

title: Scavenger_Encounter_Hostile
---
Vee: Well. If it isn't Jim.
Vee: Struck out again, I take it? Good. Keep it up.
Vee: One of these days something out here just blows you apart, and the Corporation files it as attrition.
Vee: Fewer of us, more scrap for me.
-> ...
    Vee: Yeah. That's what I thought.
===

title: Story_06_Call_Vee
---
Vee: Jim! You came through. I saw the wormhole signature light up half the band.
<<if attitude("Vee") >= 25>>
    Vee: I was starting to worry, and I don't do that for just anyone.
<<else>>
    Vee: Figured I'd check whether the new one came out the far side in one piece.
<<endif>>
Vee: First jump is the one that gets people. Half of them come out facing the wrong way and burn their whole reserve turning around.
Vee: Listen, the Corporation is going to call you. They always call right after a jump, once they know you survived the trip.
Vee: Whatever number they give you, start hauling the same hour. Do not sit on it.
-> Thanks, Vee. I owe you one.
    <<apply_effect Attitude_Vee_Plus_5>>
    Vee: Keep saying that and I'll start keeping count.
-> How long do these deadlines usually run?
    Vee: Long enough to look generous, short enough to hurt. That's the whole trick of it.
-> I can read a quota without help, Vee.
    <<apply_effect Attitude_Vee_Minus_5>>
    Vee: Right. Next time I'll let the band stay quiet.
===

title: Story_06_Call_Sid
---
Sid: So the new asset made it through a wormhole. Congratulations.
<<if attitude("Sid") <= -15>>
    Sid: I had hoped the jump would save me the trouble. Clearly it was not thorough.
<<else>>
    Sid: I had a bet running on you. I lost it. That is the extent of my interest in you.
<<endif>>
Sid: This sector was quiet before you showed up. Quiet pays. You will understand that when your quota lands.
Sid: I know your channel now. Stay off my wrecks.
-> Noted. I'll stay out of your way.
    <<apply_effect Attitude_Sid_Plus_5>>
    Sid: See that you do. Civil is cheaper for both of us.
-> Then stay out of mine, Sid.
    <<apply_effect Attitude_Sid_Minus_5>>
    Sid: Remember you said that when your hull starts leaking.
===

title: Story_07_Quota_Corp
---
<<apply_effect QuestTrigger_Quota>>
Officer: Asset. Transit confirmed. Welcome to your assigned deep field.
Officer: Your quota is now active: {quota_amount()} units of scrap.
Officer: You have {quota_sectors()} sectors to haul it, this one included. Hand it over at your intercom whenever you are carrying the full load.
Officer: At the last gate a tender takes it off you either way. If your hold is short when that gate comes, your sentence resumes on the spot.
Officer: The figure and the sectors you have left are posted on your assignment log — the panel that sits at the top of your display. It does not go away. Neither does the deadline.
Jim: Understood.
Officer: Of course it is understood. Haul well, asset.
===

title: Story_09_Upgrades_Vee
---
Vee: One more thing, since nobody at the Corporation is going to tell you.
Vee: That bench in your hold isn't decoration. It builds parts out of scrap — the same scrap you're already hauling for them.
Vee: You need the schematic first. Traders carry them, and the basic ones they practically give away, so take every one you're offered.
Vee: Then build the part at the bench and fit it on the Upgrades tab. It stays on the ship for good — one of each, so no bolting on two hooks and calling it clever.
Vee: Start with the hook. A better hook pulls the heavy wrecks, and the heavy ones are where the iron sits. Scrap keeps you breathing; iron is what you sell.
-> I'll set scrap aside for it.
    <<apply_effect Attitude_Vee_Plus_5>>
    Vee: Good. Quota first, hook second, and you stop running on fumes.
-> The quota comes first.
    Vee: It does. Just don't haul for them a whole year on the hook you started with.
===

title: Story_10_Quota_Next
---
Officer: Asset. Your last consignment is logged and weighed. The foundry has it.
Officer: A new quota is already posted on your board: {quota_amount()} units of scrap, {quota_sectors()} sectors to run it.
Officer: The terms don't soften because you met them once. Neither does what happens if you fall short.
-> Understood.
    Officer: Naturally. Haul well, asset.
-> How many of these are there?
    Officer: Your sentence is counted in cargo, not in quotas. Haul well.
===
